From: keystore-fault-investigation <kirin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] vmapple/aes: honor per-context IV selection in CMD_DATA; fix CMD_IV context mask; resync FIFO on errors

hw/vmapple/aes.c mishandles IV contexts in a matched pair of bugs:

1. cmd_data() never extracted the IV-context field from the command word
   (ctxt_iv was hardcoded to 0), so every CMD_DATA used IV context 0.

2. CMD_IV_CONTEXT_MASK was shifted by CMD_KEY_CONTEXT_SHIFT (27) instead
   of CMD_IV_CONTEXT_SHIFT (26), so an IV programmed for context N
   (command word bits 26-27) was written to context 0 as well (or, read
   as bits 27-28, would have indexed past the iv[] array; cmd_iv() had
   no bounds check).

On the current macOS guest the two bugs cancel *only while command
rounds stay strictly sequential*: the IV programmed for ctx 1 lands in
iv[0], and the following CMD_DATA (iv field = 1) reads it back from
iv[0], yielding the crypto-correct result (verified by replaying traced
rounds through openssl).  Any interleaving of the guest's two key
contexts at sub-round granularity - e.g. the AppleVPKeyStore system and
user sessions issuing concurrently on different vCPUs - makes the DATA
op of one context consume the other context's IV, producing garbage
CBC output and failing the wrapped-key integrity check: guest-side
"kek failed to unwrap vek; mix-n-match?", "failed to decode vek",
volume "unlock failed"/half-initialised APFS crypto state.  Fixing only
one of the two breaks the cancellation for the sequential case, so both
must be fixed together:

- CMD_IV writes iv[N] for a ctx-N program (mask fixed, bounds checked).
- CMD_DATA reads iv[N] for an iv-field of N.

CMD_STORE_IV used the same broken mask and now stores the correct
context's chained IV (the guest keeps a chained-IV KDF table).

Also: a command that failed hard (unsupported block mode/key length,
DMA error, qcrypto error) used to return false without resetting the
FIFO or signalling the guest, leaving a stale half-eaten command that
desynced every subsequent command and never raising an IRQ (silent
wedge -> guest "operation failed (ret: e00002bc)" storms).  The command
handlers now distinguish AES_CMD_NEED_MORE (incomplete payload: keep
the FIFO, no error) from AES_CMD_ERROR (drop the command, raise
REG_IRQ_STATUS_INVALID_CMD), and a FIFO-overflow write does the same
instead of silently discarding the word.

Verified: live boot with aes_* tracing; per-round openssl replay of the
captured unwrap commands; headless boot of -M vmapple with this patch
(keystore init, mkey unwrap, xART reads all behave; no AES command
errors).

Also included:
- vmapple.c: skip the paravirt GPU when its device type is not compiled
  in (standalone Linux builds have neither apple-gfx-mmio nor
  reims-vgpu-mmio) instead of dying in qdev_new(); vendor builds are
  unaffected because the type always exists there.
- hw/display/meson.build: skip the reims-vgpu staticlib when the sibling
  crates/reims-vgpu checkout is absent, so this tree builds standalone.

Apply in the QEMU source root:  patch -p1 < this file
diff --git a/hw/display/meson.build b/hw/display/meson.build
index 8655c94c3e..78c2061ec0 100644
--- a/hw/display/meson.build
+++ b/hw/display/meson.build
@@ -65,12 +65,24 @@ system_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_ATI_VGA', if_true: [files('ati.c', 'ati_2d.c', 'ati_
 system_ss.add(when: [pvg, 'CONFIG_MAC_PVG_PCI'],     if_true: [files('apple-gfx.m', 'apple-gfx-pci.m')])
 system_ss.add(when: [pvg, 'CONFIG_MAC_PVG_MMIO'],    if_true: [files('apple-gfx.m', 'apple-gfx-mmio.m')])
 
-if config_all_devices.has_key('CONFIG_REIMS_VGPU')
+# A standalone QEMU checkout (no reims-vgpu sibling repo) cannot build the
+# staticlib; build without the device instead of failing configure.  A
+# request for gfx-device=reims-vgpu-mmio is then rejected at runtime, and
+# -M vmapple falls back to headless (see create_gfx()).
+reims_vgpu_sibling_dir = meson.global_source_root() / '..' / '..'
+reims_vgpu_available = config_all_devices.has_key('CONFIG_REIMS_VGPU') and \
+  fs.exists(reims_vgpu_sibling_dir / 'crates' / 'reims-vgpu' / 'include' / 'reims_vgpu_qemu_abi.h')
+if config_all_devices.has_key('CONFIG_REIMS_VGPU') and not reims_vgpu_available
+  warning('reims-vgpu sibling not found at ' + reims_vgpu_sibling_dir +
+          '; building without CONFIG_REIMS_VGPU')
+endif
+
+if reims_vgpu_available
   # Thin QEMU C device links crates/reims-vgpu (Cargo staticlib). Backend is a
   # configure-time choice baked into the binary — one backend per boot, never a
   # runtime env sniff.
   # Sources live in the parent repo, two directories above this vendored submodule.
-  reims_vgpu_repo_dir = meson.global_source_root() / '..' / '..'
+  reims_vgpu_repo_dir = reims_vgpu_sibling_dir
   reims_vgpu_abi_header_dir = reims_vgpu_repo_dir / 'crates' / 'reims-vgpu' / 'include'
   # A meson option and not the environment, because meson re-runs this file
   # whenever ninja decides the build is out of date — and that regeneration
